[stderr] ## GPI-007 — reproducible editorial lifecycle
[stderr] 
[stderr] A transition input is `(cardId, currentRevision, event, evidenceRefs,
[stderr] actorRole, occurredAt)`. It emits an append-only event and either next state or
[stderr] validation failure. Time is recorded UTC and never supplies a missing
[stderr] transition. A revision is immutable after leaving `draft`; correction creates
[stderr] a successor revision under the same stable ID.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Current state | Permitted event / required evidence | Next state | Eligible |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `draft` | `submit_factual_check`: provenance, originality, accepted factual review | `fact_checked` | no |
[stderr] | `fact_checked` | `accept_accessibility_copy`: different reviewer, accepted revision review | `accessibility_edited` | no |
[stderr] | `accessibility_edited` | `accept_second_review`: distinct reviewer, grammar/alias validation | `second_reviewed` | no |
[stderr] | `second_reviewed` | `publish`: release editor, all release checks pass | `published` | yes |
[stderr] | pre-publication | `block`: dispute, missing rights, or validation failure | `blocked` | no |
[stderr] | `published` | `withdraw`: reason, effective UTC time, evidence | `withdrawn` | no |
[stderr] | `blocked` or `withdrawn` | `revise`: creates r+1 and preserves history/correction note | `draft` (r+1) | no |
[stderr] 
[stderr] No other transition is allowed: no direct draft-to-published, self-review, or
[stderr] silent reopen. Each event records from-state, to-state, card/revision, event,
[stderr] reason/evidence, actor ID/role, and UTC time. Replay from draft reproduces
[stderr] state; invalid events remain rejected audit entries and change no state.
[stderr] 
[stderr] A reported conflict blocks an unpublished card or withdraws a published card
[stderr] immediately. Withdrawal records `factual_correction`, `source_rights`,
[stderr] `ambiguity`, or `accessibility_defect`, effective time, evidence, and an
[stderr] optional replacement. It does not silently replace cards or rewrite past rounds.
[stderr] New selection excludes it from that time; a presented attempt resolves against
[stderr] its presented revision. Only a corrected, newly reviewed revision may publish.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Seeded anti-repeat and shortage policy
[stderr] 
[stderr] For `(catalogueSnapshotId, seed, requestedCount, filter, ledger)`, freeze the
[stderr] published eligible catalogue at the snapshot, apply filter, then remove
[stderr] blocked/withdrawn cards. `recent` is the union of IDs in the two latest
[stderr] *completed* rounds, sorting rounds by `(completedAtUtc, roundId)` descending.
[stderr] Draft, abandoned, and in-progress rounds do not count.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Set `fresh = candidates − recent`. Assign each candidate
[stderr] `SHA-256(seed + "|" + catalogueSnapshotId + "|" + cardId)`, then sort
[stderr] ascending by `(key, cardId)`. Take the first
[stderr] `min(requestedCount, fresh.length)`; fill only the remainder from
[stderr] `reuse = candidates ∩ recent` in the same order. Whenever reuse occurs or
[stderr] eligible cards are too few, record:
[stderr] 
[stderr] `{ requestedCount, freshAvailable, eligibleAvailable, reusedCardIds,
[stderr] unfilledCount, disclosureKey: "selection_shortage_reuse_disclosed" }`.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Show this plain-language disclosure before round start whenever reuse or an
[stderr] unfilled count exists. No clock, random source, network response, unpublished
[stderr] card, or iteration order affects selection. Equal full input has equal IDs and
[stderr] shortage record.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Eight seeded selection fixtures
[stderr] 
[stderr] Candidate lists are frozen, filtered, and published; R1/R2 are newest completed
[stderr] rounds. Implementation fixtures calculate the specified SHA-256 ordering and
[stderr] assert selected IDs, shortage fields, and disclosure key.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | ID | Snapshot / seed / count | Candidates; R1; R2 | Expected result |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | SEL-01 | `S1` / `alpha` / 2 | A,B,C,D; A; B | selected `D,C`; no reuse/shortage |
[stderr] | SEL-02 | `S1` / `alpha` / 3 | A,B,C,D; A; B | selected `D,C,B`; reused `B`; disclose |
[stderr] | SEL-03 | `S1` / `beta` / 3 | A,B,C,D; A; B | selected `D,C,A`; reused `A`; disclose |
[stderr] | SEL-04 | `S2` / `alpha` / 3 | A,B,C; A; B | selected `C,A,B`; reused `A,B`; disclose |
[stderr] | SEL-05 | `S2` / `alpha` / 4 | A,B,C; A; B | selected `C,A,B`; `unfilledCount=1`; disclose |
[stderr] | SEL-06 | `S3` / `gamma` / 2 | A,B; A; B | selected `A,B`; both reused; disclose |
[stderr] | SEL-07 | `S4` / `delta` / 2 | A,B,C; A; R2 abandoned | selected `B,C`; no reuse |
[stderr] | SEL-08 | `S5` / `epsilon` / 2 | A,B,C; A; B withdrawn | selected `C,A`; B excluded; disclose |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Release checks
[stderr] 
[stderr] Eligibility requires published frozen-snapshot state, correct answer rule, no
[stderr] alias collision, current provenance, two distinct accepted reviews, and no
[stderr] effective withdrawal. The fixture suite requires exactly ADJ-01–ADJ-20 and
[stderr] SEL-01–SEL-08. Failed validation blocks release; it cannot create a fallback
[stderr] answer or undisclosed repeat.
[stderr] ### design-evidence/question-card-and-qba-catalogue-bible.md
[stderr] # Question-card and QB-A catalogue bible
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Scope:** accepted design evidence for `GAME-001`, `GAME-002`, and
[stderr] `GPI-001`–`GPI-005`. It defines a finite editorial package, not a claim that
[stderr] the cards below are published game content. A runtime package may contain only
[stderr] the records that pass the release gate in this document.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Release boundary and original-expression rule
[stderr] 
[stderr] `QB-A` ("Kickoff Mix") is exactly the 48 stable IDs `QB-A-01` through
[stderr] `QB-A-48`, divided into four named 12-card batches. The IDs are permanent:
[stderr] a correction increments the revision and preserves the ID; a withdrawn card is
[stderr] not silently replaced. No other `QB-A-*` ID is in the first release.
[stderr] 
[stderr] The catalogue's question intents and canonical answers below are authored for
[stderr] this game. At production, the copy editor writes a fresh, concise prompt and
[stderr] explanation from the stated facts; authors must not reproduce source wording,
[stderr] answer-option sets, headlines, tables, or any reference-product expression.
[stderr] The declared sources verify facts only and grant no permission to copy their
[stderr] expression. A content record with uncertain source permission, attribution, or
[stderr] rights is `blocked`, never published.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## `GPI-001`: card grammar and release record
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every field below is required unless its condition says otherwise. Player text
[stderr] is localisable by key; answer matching uses canonical IDs and configured answer
[stderr] rules (the detailed normalisation policy is owned by `GPI-006`).
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Field | Rule / purpose |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `id`, `revision`, `batchId`, `status` | Stable ID, positive revision, one named batch, and lifecycle: `draft → fact_checked → accessibility_edited → second_reviewed → published`; alternatively `blocked` or `withdrawn`. Only `published` is eligible. |
[stderr] | `originalityRecord` | Author ID/creation date, independently-authored confirmation, and a non-copying check. It records inspiration boundaries, never reference-product text. |
[stderr] | `promptKey`, `promptText`, `instructionKey` | One independently written, single-fact prompt plus a format-appropriate, plain-language instruction. Prompt text must be answerable without an image, sound, colour, time limit, or unstated context. |
[stderr] | `format` | Exactly one of `four_option_select`, `true_false`, `ordered_pair`, or `short_typed_answer`. |
[stderr] | `category`, `topic`, `era`, `competitionScopes`, `clubIds`, `difficultyBand`, `tags` | Discovery and quota fields. `difficultyBand` is integer 1–3; all material competition/club references must be listed to enforce caps. |
[stderr] | `answerOptions` | Required only for select/true-false. Select has exactly four unique option IDs and one `correct`; true/false has canonical `true`/`false` IDs. Options are text-labelled and have non-colour selection/result states. |
[stderr] | `canonicalAnswer` and `acceptedAnswerRule` | Select/true-false use canonical option IDs; ordered pair specifies exactly two ordered canonical IDs; typed answer provides canonical display answer, normalised accepted aliases, and no fuzzy match. Empty aliases and aliases that could mean another answer are invalid. |
[stderr] | `explanationKey`, `explanationText` | Short fact-first explanation shown after resolution, including canonical answer where useful. It must explain why rather than merely say "correct". |
[stderr] | `accessibility` | `screenReaderPrompt`, `screenReaderOptions` where applicable, `answerStateText`, `explanationTextEquivalent`, reading-order/focus requirement, and text-size/contrast review. No visual or audio-only clue may be needed. |
[stderr] | `provenanceId`, `factualAssertions[]` | Links each assertion to permitted durable sources/research records, source locator, checked date, and conflict/correction history. All assertions must be supported. |
[stderr] | `reviews[]` | Two distinct completed reviews: factual/rights review and accessibility/copy review. Each carries reviewer ID/role, decision, date, findings, and revision reviewed. |
[stderr] | `localeKeys`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `withdrawal` | Externalised player strings and audit timestamps. Withdrawal requires reason, effective date, and replacement relationship only when one exists. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Format accessibility rules.** Four-option select renders every option as a
[stderr] labelled button and never uses position as the only clue. True/false spells out
[stderr] both values. Ordered pair labels first/second positions and supports reorder by
[stderr] keyboard and touch. Typed answer announces validation requirements, preserves
[stderr] entered text on rejected submission, and always exposes the canonical answer
[stderr] and explanation after resolution. Every format supports touch, pointer,
[stderr] keyboard, and gamepad-equivalent selection; no format relies on a timer.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Provenance and two-review workflow
[stderr] 
[stderr] Each inventory row references a source family below; the eventual card's
[stderr] `provenanceId` is `PROV-<card ID>` and must contain the precise page/document
[stderr] URL, retrieval date, factual-assertion mapping, and correction log before it
[stderr] can leave `draft`. The source family is a research route, not a substituted
[stderr] source record.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Key | Permitted durable primary route |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `FIFA` | FIFA competition archives, match reports, and official records |
[stderr] | `UEFA` | UEFA competition archives and official match reports/records |
[stderr] | `CONMEBOL` | CONMEBOL tournament archives and official records |
[stderr] | `AFC` | Asian Football Confederation tournament archives and official records |
[stderr] | `PL` | Premier League official history, match, and competition records |
[stderr] | `LALIGA` | LALIGA official history, match, and competition records |
[stderr] | `SERIEA` | Lega Serie A official history, match, and competition records |
[stderr] | `DFL` / `LFP` | DFL Bundesliga and Ligue de Football Professionnel official history/competition records |
[stderr] | `FF` | France Football's official Ballon d'Or records |
[stderr] | `CLUB` | The relevant club's official history/records page, cross-checked against its competition organiser where the assertion concerns a competition |
[stderr] 
[stderr] For each card revision, reviewer `R1` (factual/rights editor) verifies every
[stderr] assertion against the exact provenance locator, source permission and original
[stderr] expression record. Reviewer `R2` (accessibility/copy editor), a different
[stderr] person, verifies unambiguous language, answer options/aliases, explanation,
[stderr] screen-reader text, non-colour state wording, and localisation keys. R1's
[stderr] accepted decision moves the record to `fact_checked`; R2's accepted decision
[stderr] moves it to `second_reviewed`; only the release editor may then set
[stderr] `published`. A rejection or later dispute sets `blocked`; a published error
[stderr] sets `withdrawn` until both reviews approve a new revision. Reviewer identity,
[stderr] date, revision, decision, and finding are mandatory, making absent or duplicate
[stderr] reviews mechanically rejectable.
[stderr] 
